| 1 | [Action] | [Result] |
| 2 | [Action] | [Result] |
```

# Importing the File

After generating the CSV, tell the user how to import it:

1. Open the project in Test Manager and choose Add Test Case > Import CSV. A new, empty project shows a Drop your CSV or browse option instead.
2. Select the layout "Test cases use a single row".
3. In Map fields, check that each column maps to the field with the same name. With the header row above, Test Manager maps all nine columns automatically. Leave any other column as Ignore Field. Mapping Title is mandatory.
4. In Map values, choose `/` as the folder delimiter, and map or create any Priority or Type value the project does not have yet.
5. In Preview import, check how many test cases will be created, updated, left unchanged, or skipped. Download the skipped rows file to see the reason for each skipped row.

# Test Manager Specifics

Keep these Test Manager behaviors in mind:

1. The import accepts a valid `.csv` file of up to 10 MB. Split larger suites into several files.
2. To map a column to a custom field, create the field and link it to the project before importing, or use Add New Field during mapping.
3. Match key values are compared after trimming spaces and ignoring letter case.
4. When Title is the match key, a title that matches more than one existing test case is skipped.
5. Without a match key, every row creates a new test case, so importing the same file twice creates duplicates. With a match key, re-importing an unchanged file changes nothing.
6. Test Manager also offers a "Test cases use multiple rows" layout and a BDD Scenarios field. This rule does not use them because the documentation does not specify how those rows and cells are laid out. For behavior-driven scenarios, use a Gherkin rule.

# Test Case Writing Best Practices

1. Write titles that state the behavior under test
2. Keep steps atomic, with one action per step
3. Give every step an expected outcome, not just the last one
4. List every precondition needed to reproduce the test
5. Use concrete test data instead of vague descriptions
6. Keep test cases independent of each other
7. Use the same names for the same UI elements across all test cases
8. Cover positive, negative, and boundary cases

# Test Case Adaptation

Adapt the output to:

- The Priority, Type, and custom field values defined in the user's project
- The team's folder structure and naming conventions
- Extra columns for custom fields, each named after its field and mapped during import

